+### Last-bit differences between runs
+
+The same build, on the same input, can finish a Krylov solve in one of two states that
+differ in the last bit, from one process to the next. In the linear solves measured the
+iteration counts are the same; a strongly nonlinear solve can amplify the difference into a
+different path (a visco-elasto-plastic box step from evolved history: 39 Newton and 691
+linear iterations one way, 45 and 519 the other).
+The source is PETSc's GEMV path for `VecMDot` and `VecMAXPY`, its default here, which
+hands the multiple dot products of the Krylov orthogonalisation (and of a null-space
+projection) to the BLAS `dgemv`. Measured on a rotated free-slip shell solve: two final
+residuals, 7.380098e-08 and 7.380102e-08, in about half the runs each; with the GEMV path
+off, one value in every run. The hash seed, the thread counts and the JIT are not involved.
+
+To make a comparison between runs exact, turn the path off:
+
+```bash
+-vec_mdot_use_gemv 0 -vec_maxpy_use_gemv 0
+```
+
+(on the command line, or in `PETSC_OPTIONS`). Otherwise, before calling a last-bit
+difference between two runs a regression, check whether it goes away with these options.
+A rare flip needs many fresh processes to show: one in 40 in a bare petsc4py replay.
+
 ## The multigrid option bundle, and its one owner
 
 Three routes reach a multigrid velocity block, and they are **the same
